name: dotnet-channels

High-performance thread-safe producer/consumer queues using `System.Threading.Channels`: `Channel<T>`, bounded/unbounded channels, backpressure, capacity, `IAsyncEnumerable`, graceful shutdown/draining, hosted services. See [skill:dotnet-background-services] and [skill:dotnet-csharp-async-patterns].

`Channel<T>` has separate thread-safe `ChannelWriter<T>` and `ChannelReader<T>` endpoints. `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` provides back-pressure and capped memory; `Channel.CreateUnbounded<T>()` grows without limit and can exhaust memory under load. Bounded channels are preferred for production; unbounded channels are for guaranteed-low-volume/prototyping or when you control the producer rate.

`FullMode`: `Wait` blocks `WriteAsync`; `DropOldest` drops oldest; `DropNewest` and `DropWrite` drop the item being written; `DropWrite` returns `false` from `TryWrite`.

Starting in .NET 7, bounded drop-mode channels accept an `itemDropped` callback for metrics, logging, or resource cleanup. It receives the dropped item and `ChannelWriter<T>`.

await writer.WriteAsync(item, cancellationToken);
if (!writer.TryWrite(item))

Multiple producers can call `WriteAsync` or `TryWrite` concurrently without external locking. Call `Complete()` or `TryComplete()` when no more items will be produced. `TryComplete` is idempotent—safe to call multiple times.

writer.TryComplete(new InvalidOperationException("Source failed"));

Preferred consumer pattern:
while (await reader.WaitToReadAsync(cancellationToken))
while (reader.TryRead(out var item))
await ProcessAsync(item, cancellationToken);

This drains available items before awaiting again. Multiple consumer tasks scale processing; each item is read by exactly one consumer. `ReadAllAsync()` returns an `IAsyncEnumerable<T>`:
